Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> On Sun, Oct 10, 2010 at 2:12 PM, Robert LeChevalier <lojbab@lojban.org> w=
rote:
>=20
>>Actually, it would have to be fa'o lo zarci to be sure to end any prior t=
ext
>>before starting the new one.
>=20
>=20
> This is the first time I hear about that. I'm pretty sure that the
> standard line until now was that anything after fa'o was to be
> ignored.

It is ignored.  It is not part of the same text.

It may be part of a different text, though.  The parser doesn't care=20
about any texts other than the one it is parsing.

>> But then for most practical purposes, .i or
>>ni'o are good enough.
>=20
> But they are not. The two texts ".i mi gleki" and ".i go'i ra'o"
> cannot be concatenated into one text without loss of meaning. The
> single text ".i mi gleki .i go'i ra'o" does not have the same meaning
> as the exchange of texts ".i mi gleki", ".i go'i ra'o".

That is because the word ra'o has metalinguistic effect that overrides=20
the textual concatenation.

>>"Back in the day" (makes me feel elderly to use that
>>phrase), we thought that each exchange in formal Lojban conversation woul=
d
>>generally involve starting with .i or ni'o (recognizing that informally i=
t
>>would usually be left out).
>=20
>=20
> The use of initial .i/ni'o makes little difference to this issue.

It does when the people conversing are using sentence fragments.

If I ask  "ma klama"

and you answer "mi", then we need at least a convention/protocol to know=20
whether that means the unbroken text "ma klama mi"  or "ma klama [.i] mi=20
[go'i]".  If the default is endless unbroken text (until an explicit=20
fa'o, .i, or ni'o), then in most actual Lojban usage, the sumti that=20
occur before a selbri would be understood as appending onto the end of=20
the last bridi spoken by the prior speaker.

We probably don't want that because in actual usage, people have NOT=20
typically started their conversation turns with .i or ni'o.  The=20
solution has to be metalinguistic; generally an implicit default=20
protocol with explicit ways to override that default, which is why I=20
suggested using either SEI or COI constructs.

lojbab

